# Line endings are pinned, not left to each clone's core.autocrlf.
#
# Without this file, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true checks .ps1 and .sh
# files out as CRLF. The .sh case breaks any hook run through a POSIX shell (a
# `\r` on the shebang line, or trailing every command). The .ps1 case is subtler:
# tests/framework-checks.sh treats `\r` as a non-ASCII byte, so the whole suite
# goes red on a file whose actual content is clean -- and CONTRIBUTING.md's first
# instruction is to run that suite, so a new contributor sees a failure they did
# not cause. A check that cries wolf trains people to ignore it, which is the
# failure mode framework-upgrade.md names better than anyone.
#
# PowerShell wants CRLF and POSIX shells want LF, so pin each to what it needs.

*           text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf
*.psd1      text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gif       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.ico       binary
